NITC 3D Map - Architecture Documentation
📋 Overview
This project is a 3D interactive campus map application built with React and Three.js. It allows users to explore a 3D model of the NITC campus, view building labels, click on location markers to see events, and navigate the map intuitively.

🏗️ Project Structure
nitc-map/
├── config.js                    # Configuration & data constants
├── sceneSetup.js               # Three.js scene initialization
├── modelLoader.js              # 3D model loading & processing
├── markerSystem.js             # Location marker management
├── buildingLabels.js           # Building highlighting & labels
├── cameraUtils.js              # Camera animation & constraints
├── interactionHandlers.js      # User input handling
├── animationLoop.js            # Main render loop
├── cleanupUtils.js             # Resource disposal
├── NITCMapPage.jsx             # Main React component
└── EventPanel.jsx              # UI panel component (not refactored)
📦 Module Breakdown
1. config.js - Configuration & Data
Purpose: Centralized configuration and data storage

Contents:

CONFIG: All configurable parameters (camera, controls, animations, etc.)
LOCATION_DATA: Campus locations with coordinates
EVENT_DATA: Events linked to locations
Why separate: Makes it easy to adjust settings and add new locations/events without touching code logic.

2. sceneSetup.js - Scene Initialization
Purpose: Sets up the Three.js environment

Key Functions:

initializeScene(): Creates scene, camera, renderer, and controls
setupLighting(): Adds ambient, hemisphere, and directional lights
createGroundPlane(): Creates shadow-receiving ground
Why separate: Scene setup is a one-time initialization process that doesn't need to be mixed with dynamic logic.

3. modelLoader.js - Model Loading
Purpose: Handles 3D model loading and terrain queries

Key Functions:

loadModel(): Loads GLTF model with DRACO compression
findGroundHeight(): Uses raycasting to find terrain height at X,Z
checkCollisionAtPosition(): Detects camera collisions with buildings
Why separate: Model loading is asynchronous and requires special handling. Terrain queries are used throughout the app.

4. markerSystem.js - Location Markers
Purpose: Manages 3D marker pins on the map

Key Functions:

createMarkerMaterials(): Creates shared materials for efficiency
createMarkers(): Generates markers for all locations
highlightMarker(): Highlights/unhighlights markers
findMarkerById(): Looks up markers by location ID
Why separate: Marker management is a distinct feature with its own lifecycle and interactions.

5. buildingLabels.js - Building Highlighting
Purpose: Creates floating labels and highlights buildings

Key Functions:

highlightBuildings(): Sets up building grouping, labels, and highlight system
cleanupBuildingLabels(): Removes DOM labels on unmount
Features:

Groups building meshes by name
Creates DOM overlay for labels
Applies subtle glow effect to highlighted buildings
Updates label positions every frame based on camera view
Why separate: Complex system involving both Three.js and DOM manipulation. Self-contained feature.

6. cameraUtils.js - Camera Control
Purpose: Handles camera animations and constraints

Key Functions:

clampToMapBounds(): Keeps camera within map boundaries
flyToLocation(): Animates camera to a specific point
updateCameraAnimation(): Smoothly interpolates camera position
applyCameraConstraints(): Prevents camera from going underground
resetCamera(): Returns to default view
Why separate: Camera logic is complex and used in multiple places. Centralizing it prevents duplication.

7. interactionHandlers.js - User Input
Purpose: Processes all user interactions

Key Functions:

setupEventHandlers(): Attaches all event listeners
cleanupEventHandlers(): Removes event listeners
Handles:

Mouse clicks and touch events
Double-tap to fly
Swipe/pan detection
Marker clicking
Keyboard shortcuts (R to reset)
Window resize
Why separate: Input handling is event-driven and distinct from rendering logic. Keeps event logic organized.

8. animationLoop.js - Render Loop
Purpose: Main animation loop that runs every frame

Key Functions:

startAnimationLoop(): Starts the render loop
stopAnimationLoop(): Stops the render loop
Per-frame tasks:

Update camera animation
Update controls (damping)
Apply terrain constraints
Update building labels
Render scene
Why separate: The render loop is the heartbeat of the app. Keeping it clean and focused improves performance debugging.

9. cleanupUtils.js - Resource Disposal
Purpose: Properly disposes of Three.js resources

Key Functions:

disposeScene(): Recursively disposes geometries and materials
disposeMarkers(): Cleans up marker meshes
cleanup(): Main cleanup function
Why separate: Memory management is critical for Three.js apps. Centralizing disposal logic prevents memory leaks.

10. NITCMapPage.jsx - Main Component
Purpose: Orchestrates all modules and manages React state

Responsibilities:

Initializes all modules in correct order
Manages React state for UI (panels, active location)
Provides callback functions to modules
Handles UI button clicks
Cleans up on unmount
Why it's the main file: React components need to manage lifecycle and state. This component acts as the "conductor" for all other modules.

🔄 Data Flow
User Interaction
      ↓
interactionHandlers.js (detects click/tap)
      ↓
NITCMapPage.jsx (callback triggered)
      ↓
React State Update (setPanelView, setActiveLocation)
      ↓
UI Re-renders (EventPanel shown)
      ↓
User clicks "Navigate"
      ↓
cameraUtils.js (flyToLocation)
      ↓
animationLoop.js (updates camera every frame)
      ↓
Camera reaches destination
🎯 Key Design Decisions
1. Why use refs instead of state for Three.js objects?
Three.js objects are mutable and updated every frame
Storing them in React state would cause unnecessary re-renders
Refs provide direct access without triggering React's update cycle
2. Why separate modules instead of one big file?
Maintainability: Each module has a single responsibility
Testability: Modules can be tested independently
Reusability: Functions can be used in different contexts
Readability: Easier to understand and navigate
3. Why use window globals for building labels?
DOM labels live outside React's component tree
Animation loop needs to update labels every frame
Global functions provide simple access without prop drilling
4. Why use callbacks instead of events?
Direct callbacks are simpler and more predictable
Easier to debug than event emitters
Type-safe (with TypeScript)
🚀 Adding New Features
Adding a new location:
Add entry to LOCATION_DATA in config.js
Marker will be automatically created
Adding a new event:
Add entry to EVENT_DATA in config.js
Link to location via locationId
Changing camera behavior:
Modify settings in CONFIG.CAMERA in config.js
Adjust functions in cameraUtils.js if needed
Adding new interactions:
Add handler in interactionHandlers.js
Wire up callback in NITCMapPage.jsx
🐛 Debugging Tips
Labels not showing: Check browser console for errors, verify window.updateBuildingLabels exists
Camera going underground: Adjust MIN_HEIGHT_ABOVE_TERRAIN in config
Markers in wrong place: Verify coordinates in LOCATION_DATA
Performance issues: Check animation loop, reduce label update frequency
Memory leaks: Verify cleanup functions are called on unmount
📝 Code Conventions
Function naming: Use descriptive verbs (e.g., createMarkers, handleClick)
Comments: Every module and key function has explanatory comments
Constants: Use UPPER_SNAKE_CASE for config constants
File naming: Use camelCase for .js files, PascalCase for .jsx components
Organization: Group related functions within modules
🔧 Future Improvements
TypeScript: Add type safety
Lazy loading: Load model chunks on demand
LOD system: Level-of-detail for performance
Search: Add location search feature
Routing: Generate paths between locations
Analytics: Track user interactions
Accessibility: Improve keyboard navigation
